  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🏷️ Epithet Generator",
      "tagline": "Type your character's name and find the epithet that sticks: the Bold, Dragonslayer, or something history would recognise."
    },
    "intro": "An epithet is the part of a name people add once they know you: <em>the Bold</em>, <em>Dragonslayer</em>, <em>of the North</em>. It says what you did, what you're like or where you're from, and it often outlives everything else about you. Type your character's name into this **epithet generator**, pick a category (or leave it on a mix) and see what fits.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Epithets from history",
        "content": "<p>Real kings and warriors collected some wonderful epithets, and many were far from flattering. The <strong>Historical</strong> category draws on them:</p><ul><li><strong>Æthelred the Unready</strong> (England, d. 1016): \"unready\" comes from Old English <em>unræd</em>, \"ill-advised\", a pun on his name, which means \"noble counsel\".</li><li><strong>Harald Bluetooth</strong> (Denmark): the Bluetooth wireless standard is named after him, and its logo combines the runes of his initials.</li><li><strong>Edward Longshanks</strong> (Edward I of England): for his height. <strong>John Lackland</strong>, a youngest son, was expected to inherit no lands of his own.</li><li><strong>Eric Bloodaxe</strong>, <strong>Sweyn Forkbeard</strong> and <strong>Thorfinn Skull-splitter</strong>: the <a href=\"viking-name-generator.html\">Viking</a> world's bynames are some of the best there are.</li><li><strong>Charles the Bald</strong>, <strong>Charles the Fat</strong> and <strong>Charles the Simple</strong>: three Frankish kings, three blunt verdicts. Their relative <strong>Louis the Stammerer</strong> fared no better.</li><li><strong>Henry the Fowler</strong> (Germany): the story goes he was out setting bird snares when news came that he'd been chosen king.</li><li><strong>Ivan the Terrible</strong>: the Russian <em>Grozny</em> is closer to \"formidable\" or \"fearsome\" than \"bad\".</li></ul><p>Using a real epithet makes a character feel grounded in history, and a funny one (the Fat, the Unready) can make a ruler instantly memorable.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "The, of, or nothing at all",
        "content": "<p>Epithets come in a few shapes, and the shape changes the feel:</p><ul><li><strong>the + word</strong>: <em>the Bold</em>, <em>the <a href=\"hammer-name-generator.html\">Hammer</a></em>, <em>the Wise</em>. The classic form, and the most common.</li><li><strong>A single compound word</strong>: <em>Dragonslayer</em>, <em>Lionheart</em>, <em>Stormborn</em>. These drop the \"the\" and read almost like a surname.</li><li><strong>of + place</strong>: <em>of the North</em>, <em>of the Mountains</em>. Where someone's from, especially once they've left it.</li></ul><p>The generator adds \"the\" where it belongs, so <em>Aldric the Brave</em>, <em>Aldric Dragonslayer</em> and <em>Aldric of the North</em> all come out right.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Epithets are given, not chosen",
        "content": "<p>Almost nobody picks their own epithet. Other people give it to them, and that's where the fun is:</p><ul><li><strong>It can be unfair.</strong> One bad day can stick for ever. Jaime Lannister is \"Kingslayer\" to everyone in Westeros, whatever his reasons.</li><li><strong>It can be ironic.</strong> A giant called <em>the Small</em>, a coward called <em>the Brave</em>.</li><li><strong>It can change.</strong> A character might start as <em>the Exile</em> and end as <em>the Returned</em>, which makes a good arc for a long campaign.</li><li><strong>Different people use different ones.</strong> Allies say <em>the Bold</em>; enemies say <em>the Reckless</em>.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Choosing the right category",
